{
  "concept": "The area under a curve on [a, b] is defined as the limit of sums of inscribed rectangle areas: as the number of rectangles n grows, the staircase approximation converges to the exact area.",
  "figureType": "line_plot",
  "elements": [
    "Three side-by-side panels sharing identical axes and curve, labeled (a), (b), (c) beneath each panel",
    "x-axis drawn as a horizontal arrow with italic label 'x' at the right end",
    "y-axis drawn as a vertical arrow with italic label 'y' at the top",
    "Smooth blue cubic-like curve that rises from the left, peaks left of center, dips to a local minimum right of center, then rises again toward the right edge; the curve extends slightly beyond a and b on both sides",
    "Tick labels 'a' (left) and 'b' (right) on the x-axis marking the interval endpoints",
    "Thin vertical boundary segments from the x-axis up to the curve at x = a and x = b",
    "Panel (a): entire region between curve and x-axis on [a, b] filled pale blue — the exact area",
    "Panel (b): six wide inscribed rectangles with pale-blue fill and thin black outlines, tops touching the curve at one sample point per subinterval, visible white gaps/overshoot between rectangle tops and the curve",
    "Panel (c): roughly twenty narrow inscribed rectangles with the same fill and outlines, tops hugging the curve so gaps are nearly invisible",
    "Readout showing n, the Riemann sum value, the exact area, and the absolute error",
    "Caption labels (a), (b), (c) in italic parentheses"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "rectangleCount",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Number of rectangles n",
      "range": [1, 100, 1],
      "default": 6,
      "teaches": "Increasing n shrinks each rectangle's width and the leftover white slivers between the staircase and the curve, driving the Riemann sum toward the exact area."
    },
    {
      "id": "viewMode",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "View",
      "options": ["Three panels (a)(b)(c)", "Single animated panel"],
      "default": "Three panels (a)(b)(c)",
      "teaches": "The three textbook panels are not three different pictures but three frames of one continuous refinement process.",
      "default_note": "Three panels"
    },
    {
      "id": "sampleRule",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Rectangle height taken at",
      "options": ["Left endpoint", "Midpoint", "Right endpoint"],
      "default": "Midpoint",
      "teaches": "The choice of sample point changes whether the sum over- or under-shoots for small n, but all choices converge to the same limit — so the limit, not the rule, defines the area."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The exact region",
      "narration": "Panel (a) shows the region we want to measure: everything between the blue curve and the x-axis from a to b. There is no elementary formula for this shape.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Three panels (a)(b)(c)", "rectangleCount": 1, "sampleRule": "Midpoint" }
    },
    {
      "title": "A coarse approximation",
      "narration": "Switch to one panel and drop in six rectangles whose tops touch the curve. The staircase already resembles the region, but obvious white gaps and overhangs remain — this is panel (b).",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single animated panel", "rectangleCount": 6, "sampleRule": "Midpoint" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Refining the partition",
      "narration": "Push n upward and watch each rectangle narrow. The error readout falls quickly even though the total shaded area barely moves — the staircase is closing on the curve, not growing.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single animated panel", "rectangleCount": 20, "sampleRule": "Midpoint" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Does the sample point matter?",
      "narration": "At n = 20 switch the rectangle heights to left endpoints: individual bars shift up or down, yet the total changes only slightly. The rule of choosing heights is not what determines the answer.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single animated panel", "rectangleCount": 20, "sampleRule": "Left endpoint" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Toward the limit",
      "narration": "At n = 100 the rectangles are visually indistinguishable from the shaded region of panel (a), and left, midpoint, and right sums agree to several decimals. That common limiting value is defined to be the area.",
      "state": { "viewMode": "Single animated panel", "rectangleCount": 100, "sampleRule": "Left endpoint" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The rectangles never equal the area — the area is what their total is forced to approach as the rectangles become infinitely thin, which is exactly what the definite integral means.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Three equal-width panels in a row on white, each with a thin black arrowed x-axis and y-axis meeting at an origin placed left of a. A single medium-weight cyan/sky-blue curve (about 3px) is identical in all three panels: it enters low at the left, rises to a rounded maximum about a third of the way across, falls to a shallow local minimum about three quarters across, then climbs again and exits past b. Shaded regions and rectangles use a pale desaturated blue fill; rectangles have hairline black outlines and sit on the x-axis with tops meeting the curve. Panel (a) is one continuous fill; panel (b) has about six wide bars with clearly visible mismatch at the tops; panel (c) has about twenty thin bars that visually hug the curve. Italic serif labels: 'y' at the top of each vertical axis, 'x' at the right of each horizontal axis, 'a' and 'b' under the interval endpoints, and italic '(a)', '(b)', '(c)' centered below each panel. In single-panel mode, expand one panel to full width and show a small readout in the upper-right corner with n, the Riemann sum, the exact area, and the error, with the leftover gap slivers between staircase and curve tinted a contrasting warm color so the shrinking error is visible."
}

